CHAP1_TITLE = "CHAPTER 1\nINTRODUCTION"

CHAP1_INTRO_PARAS = [
    "Rural local governance is an essential part of administration in India. Under the 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, the Panchayati Raj system was established to give local self-government powers to rural communities. The Gram Panchayat is the grassroots administrative body that serves village residents directly. In India, there are more than 250,000 Gram Panchayats managing civic services and local development for about 65% of the country's population. The Gram Panchayat is responsible for many daily administrative tasks such as collecting property and water taxes, maintaining land and ownership records, resolving public grievances, maintaining streetlights and water supply, and helping villagers access state and central government welfare schemes such as PM-Kisan, housing subsidies, and employment guarantees.",
    "Even today, most Gram Panchayats manage their daily work using traditional paper registers. Panchayat Development Officers (PDOs), village accountants, and clerical staff record tax payments, complaint entries, citizen records, and meeting minutes manually in physical books. While this manual system has been used for a long time, it causes many practical difficulties. Paper registers can get easily damaged by moisture, rain, pests, or accidental fire. Over time, pages tear, ink fades, and locating older tax receipts or complaint records takes a lot of time. When records are stored in separate files across different desks, it is difficult to verify whether a resident has paid past taxes or if an issue reported months ago was properly resolved.",
    "This manual process also creates serious inconvenience for rural citizens. People living in distant wards have to travel several kilometers to reach the Panchayat office during working hours. When they arrive, officials may be busy with field inspections, meetings, or auditing physical files. As a result, citizens often have to visit the office multiple times, wait in long queues, spend money on local travel, and lose their daily wages just to check their tax dues, pay a bill, or submit a complaint. Furthermore, because there is no single system connecting tax collection, citizen complaints, and welfare schemes, villagers often miss out on government benefits they are eligible for. To overcome these problems, we developed the \"Smart Rural App – Smart Gram Panchayat System\". It is a web-based digital platform designed to bring core Panchayat services online, making local governance simple, transparent, and accessible to everyone in the village."
]

CHAP1_PROBLEM_STATEMENT = [
    "The traditional administration in rural local bodies faces several practical problems because there is no single digital system to manage records and services. In a typical Gram Panchayat, everyday work depends heavily on physical ledgers and in-person visits, leading to the following issues:",
    "1. Damage and Loss of Paper Records: All basic village records, such as property registers, tax demand registers, citizen details, and complaint books, are kept on paper. These files can be easily damaged by water, insects, or normal wear and tear, and misplacing a file can cause loss of important information.",
    "2. Delay and Revenue Loss in Tax Collection: Property tax is the main source of income for a Gram Panchayat to maintain local roads, streetlights, and drinking water. In manual bookkeeping, it is hard for staff to track who has paid and who has not. Without automated calculation and reminder systems, many taxpayers miss deadlines, leading to accumulated pending dues that reduce the Panchayat's funds.",
    "3. Slow and Unorganized Complaint Handling: When citizens face civic issues such as broken water pipelines, faulty streetlights, or drainage problems, they usually register complaints verbally or on paper. There is no automated way to categorize these complaints or highlight urgent issues like water leakage or electrical hazards, and citizens have no way to track whether their complaint is being addressed.",
    "4. Lack of Awareness About Government Schemes: Many central and state welfare programs are available for farmers, women, and low-income families, but rural citizens are often unaware of them. Information is usually displayed on office notice boards or distant government portals that are hard for villagers to access, so eligible families miss out on benefits.",
    "5. Language and Digital Barriers: Most existing government software systems are designed in English with complex interfaces that are hard for rural citizens to use on their own. Without regional language options (such as Kannada) and simple communication channels, villagers struggle to use online services.",
    "These problems show the clear need for a centralized, easy-to-use web application that digitizes property taxes, handles complaints systematically, shares government schemes, and provides bilingual support to villagers."
]

